From: nerves_system_fp3 <devs@spin42.com>
Date: Sat, 17 May 2026 10:30:00 +0200
Subject: arm64: dts: qcom: fp3p: bump cam-vaf/cam-vio startup delays

The cam_vaf (2.85V via GPIO 128) and cam_vio (1.8V via GPIO 130)
regulators had startup-delay-us = 200, which is the bare minimum a
fixed-regulator driver would honour after the enable GPIO goes high.
With that timing the AK7374 VCM at I²C address 0x0c never acks the
first write the upstream ak7375 driver issues from its resume hook:

  ak7375 3-000c: ak7375_vcm_resume I2C failure: -6

200µs isn't enough for the rails to actually stabilise before we
start I²C transactions on the VCM. Empirically the chip is alive (a
manual `i2cdetect`-style probe done shortly after the driver gives
up returns sensible position bytes), so power is just slow to come
up. Bumping both delays to 10ms gives the chip time to settle and
matches what the downstream Qualcomm camera_v2 sensor power-up
sequence does for this board.

Signed-off-by: nerves_system_fp3 <devs@spin42.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts
@@ -105,7 +105,7 @@
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 		gpio = <&tlmm 130 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		enable-active-high;
-		startup-delay-us = <200>;
+		startup-delay-us = <10000>;
 	};

 	/* Rear camera AF actuator supply, active-high enable on GPIO 128 */
@@ -116,7 +116,7 @@
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <2850000>;
 		gpio = <&tlmm 128 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		enable-active-high;
-		startup-delay-us = <200>;
+		startup-delay-us = <10000>;
 	};

 	/* Front camera DVDD, active-high enable on GPIO 46 */
